Faraday's paradox, or in other words, the question "Do magnetic field lines rotate with the magnet or not?" still hasn’t been definitively answered, as far as I know...
The problem lies in the experimental setup. In the closed circuit approach, when the inductor rotates with the magnet (assuming the magnetic field is stationary), then two opposing, canceling forces (voltages) could be induced. So, the result is EMF = 0.
OR nothing is induced at all because the field rotates along with the magnet, there’s no relative motion, and EMF is again zero.
We have no way of knowing which explanation is correct.

There’s also the E.H Kennard experiment and this is the open circuit approach (spinning open capacitor), but the induced voltage is so small that the setup requires a very sensitive amplifier. Kennard’s experiment is disliked by relativists (yes, Einstein, that guy again), because according to relativistic electromagnetism, the magnetic field should rotate with the magnet. They argue that the tiny currents observed in the Kennard experiment are not from a stationary field.

After reading many papers and seeing many experiments that failed to prove or disprove the field rotation theory, I designed my own original experiment. My idea uses “hollow discs and brushes” and operates in a closed circuit so no tiny currents. The prototype is almost ready, I will publish a video when it’s done and start a channel.

There’s more; in the case of Earth, the magnetic field is tilted and not aligned with the planet’s rotational axis. That’s why we have both true (geographic) north and magnetic north.
The Earth’s magnetic field has a kind of "wobble" and a very low frequency of movement.
If the magnetic field does not rotate with the magnet (which is what I’m trying to prove with my device), then in Earth’s case, we may be dealing with two components of the magnetic field:
1.one part rotates (in a wobbling motion)
2.and the other part remains stationary (at the center of Earth’s core).

Of course, the Earth’s magnetic field is very weak, and the planet rotates slowly but there was a man, Lester Hendershot, who claimed to have invented a free energy device back in the 1930s. It supposedly generated energy from the Earth’s magnetic field. The device somehow utilized the Earth rotation and difference between magnetic north and true north, an magnetic gear effect, conversion from low frequency and weak field to high frequency.
According to legend, Lester discovered this effect by accident while trying to improve an induction compass. The so-called Hendershot generator was hard to tune, and eventually, a frustrated Hendershot took his own life. Sceptics say that it was a hoax.

I find it very odd that the scientific community is no longer interested in the Faraday paradox anymore.

