See the Faraday homopolar generator paradox from 1851 under Faraday paradox in Wikipedia.  Rotate a conductive disk on an axial fixed permanent magnet and you make voltage.  Rotate the magnet axially instead (so the same relative motion between the disk and the magnet) and you get nothing, which seems wrong since the "relative motion" between the field lines and disk are the same.  The reason it's not a paradox is the "lines" don't rotate when you axially rotate the magnet - the lines are not real, just a intuitive construct that are physically meaningless.  The rest of the article is useful for understanding why this paper is controversial.

It should be noted that ferrites are notoriously nonlinear materials, especially looking at the conductivity of a bunch of grains sintered together (think of a material made out of billions of cat's whiskers detector diodes massed together in an ensemble), and have the potential to slightly rectify ambient AC magnetic fields through this mechanism.  An alternative test would be a shell of mu metal rather than conductive ferrite, which is both more permeable and has metallic conductivity throughout.  There is still the potential for nonlinearity from magnetic effects in the mu metal (see magnetic reactors and flux gates), but at least nonlinearity in electrical conductivity would be eliminated.



